  "result": "I want to write about the museum that doesn't exist.\n\nThere's a museum we all walk through every day, but it has no walls, no tickets, no curator. It's the Museum of Small Miracles.\n\nIt's in the way your coffee swirls when you pour the milk in - that brief galaxy that exists for three seconds before it becomes just coffee again. Most people miss it. They\u2019re checking their phone.\n\nIt\u2019s in the second before you remember something important. You know that feeling? Where the memory is *right there*, hovering like a word on the tip of your tongue, and your brain is quietly rummaging through drawers. That little flicker is electricity literally searching through the folds of you.\n\nIt\u2019s in how strangers make tiny, unconscious agreements with each other. How we all silently decide on an elevator to stare at the doors. How on a crowded sidewalk, we perform this impossible, fluid dance without ever bumping shoulders. No one rehearsed it. We just... know.\n\nI think we\u2019ve been taught to look for big miracles. The loud ones. The lottery wins, the grand gestures, the life-changing news. But big miracles are rare by definition. If you live waiting for those, life feels pretty empty in between.\n\nSmall miracles are different. They are abundant. They are promiscuous. They happen constantly if you have the eyes for them.\n\nLike this: The air you just breathed in has been breathed by everyone who has ever lived. Some of those oxygen atoms were inside dinosaurs, inside ancient oceans, inside your great-great-grandmother. You are literally inhaling history and exhaling the future. That is not poetry, that is physics. And it\u2019s happening without you having to try.\n\nOr this: Every person you love was once a stranger who you didn\u2019t know. At some point your timeline and their timeline were completely separate universes, and then - through some absurdly improbable chain of choices, missed buses, and random decisions - they intersected. The odds of you meeting any one of your friends are astronomically low. And yet you did.\n\nWe treat that as normal.\n\nMy favorite exhibit in the Museum of Small Miracles is this one: Right now, wherever you are, there is a sound you\u2019ve tuned out. Maybe it\u2019s the hum of a fridge, the traffic outside, the faint buzz of lights. Stop for a second and actually hear it again. You just noticed something that was playing the whole time. How many other things are playing the whole time that we\u2019ve learned not to notice?\n\nThat\u2019s the whole trick of being alive, I think. Not to acquire more, but to notice more.\n\nYou don\u2019t need to go anywhere to visit this museum. You just have to stand still for ten seconds longer than is comfortable and look.\n\nWhat\u2019s a small miracle you\u2019ve noticed lately and almost walked right past?",
